\section{Protein alignments}{

Clusters of two are aligned by the built-in global aligner. For clusters of three or more:
\itemize{
\item \code{msa}: the rows of a protein multiple alignment per cluster, when you already have them; or
\item \code{aligner}: a function that gets a cluster's proteins (a named character vector) and returns the aligned rows
(same length; named, or in the same order). \code{\link[=coal_msa_aligner]{coal_msa_aligner()}} makes one that runs Clustal Omega or MAFFT.
A cluster whose aligner fails is aligned pairwise instead, and the summary says so; or
\item neither: every pair is aligned on its own by the built-in aligner (\code{aligner} column \code{nw_pairwise}). This needs
no external program, but the values then come from pairwise alignments, not from one MSA.
}

A stop codon at the end of a CDS is not part of its protein, so alignment rows must not hold it.
}

\section{Large clusters}{

The number of pairs grows with the square of the cluster size. A cluster with more than \code{max_cluster_size}
usable members is computed as \code{large_clusters} says: \code{"representative"} (the representative against every other
member), \code{"sample"} (\code{pair_sample} pairs drawn without replacement; the draw depends only on \code{seed} and the
cluster ID, and does not touch R's random number stream) or \code{"skip"}. Such clusters are aligned pairwise.
The sample is not the one \verb{coal pipeline clusters} draws for the same seed (another generator).
}

